        "summary": {
          "en-US": "Numeric passwords compatible with openssl passwd -6"
        },
        "description": {
          "en-US": "numpwd keeps a list of key names and turns a key name plus a password into a string of digits. The digits are exactly what this shell pipeline prints when you type the same password at its prompt:\n\n<pre>openssl passwd -6 -salt KEYNAME | tr -dc '[0-9]'</pre>\n\nSo you can always recompute them on any computer with OpenSSL, without the app.\n\n<ul>\n<li>Only key names are stored. The password is never saved, logged or offered to autofill.</li>\n<li>The password and result dialogs block screenshots and screen recording.</li>\n<li>Copy button; the clipboard is cleared after 30 seconds and the result closes after a minute.</li>\n<li>Tested against hundreds of vectors generated by the openssl binary, including UTF-8 names, names longer than 16 bytes, \"$\" and \"rounds=N$\" in names, and long passwords.</li>\n<li>No permissions, no network.</li>\n</ul>\n\nNote: tr keeps '[' and ']' too, because the brackets are part of its character set, so a key name that contains brackets yields them in the output as well.\n\nnumpwd is a fork of FreeOTP (Apache License 2.0).\n"
        },
